# Shadcn UI Best Practices

- Prefer existing primitives from `@/components/ui/*` before creating new base UI components.
- Keep components single-purpose and composable; split large views into smaller modules.
- Put reusable primitives in `components/ui`; place domain-specific components by feature (e.g., `components/forms`).
- Use PascalCase for component files and camelCase for functions/variables.

## Component Usage

- Prefer variants and `className` composition over custom CSS files.
- Use `cn` from `@/lib/utils` for conditional classes; avoid manual class string concatenation.
- Keep behavior in hooks/container components and keep UI components mostly presentational.
- For forms, use accessible patterns: labels, descriptions, errors, and `aria-invalid` when applicable.

## Anti-patterns To Avoid

- Duplicating existing shadcn primitives for style-only differences.
- Heavy one-off CSS overrides instead of Tailwind utilities/variants.
- Components with too many responsibilities or deeply nested conditional rendering.
- Directly handling unsafe HTML or exposing secrets in client-side code.

## Performance, Security, and Testing

- Lazy-load non-critical routes/components when it improves initial load time.
- Clean up timers, subscriptions, and listeners in `useEffect`.
- Use memoization APIs only when profiling shows real render bottlenecks.
- Validate/sanitize user input and use schema validation (e.g., Zod) for forms and API boundaries.
- Add unit/integration tests for critical flows and accessibility behavior.

## Preferred Import Pattern

```tsx
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils"

export function SaveButton({ busy }: { busy?: boolean }) {
  return (
    <Button type="submit" disabled={busy} className={cn("w-full", busy && "opacity-80")}>
      {busy ? "Saving..." : "Save"}
    </Button>
  )
}
```
